timeline
    title From the Buttonwood tree to the matching engine
    section Manual market
        1792 : Buttonwood Agreement<br/>24 stockbrokers, 17 May : fixed commissions, closed club
        1817 : Constitution of 8 March creates<br/>the New York Stock and Exchange Board : 30 securities, called from a podium
        1867 : Stock ticker invented : market data becomes a product
        1878 : Telephones installed at the NYSE
        1886 : First one-million-share day, 15 December
    section Regulated market
        1934 : Congress creates the SEC : exchange self-regulation gets a supervisor
        1971 : Nasdaq opens as a quotation display : dealers quote, they do not match
        1975 : Congress mandates a national market system : Section 11A of the Exchange Act
        1978 : Consolidated Tape Association operating : one printed tape from many venues
        1988 : Market-wide circuit breaker rules adopted : response to October 1987
    section Electronic market
        1997 : Order handling rules put ECN quotes on the tape
        1998 : Regulation ATS creates the legal dark pool
        2001 : Decimalisation : the tick goes from 1/16 dollar to one cent
        2005 : Regulation NMS adopted 9 June : Rule 611 protects the top of every book
        2007 : Rule 610 and 611 trading phase begins 5 March : full compliance completed 8 October : NYSE hybrid market, the floor stops being the only path
    section Fragmented market
        2010 : Flash crash, 6 May : 20,000 trades broken across 300+ securities
        2013 : Limit Up Limit Down rolls out : price bands replace stub quotes
        2016 : IEX registers as an exchange : a 350-microsecond speed bump
        2020 : Four market-wide halts in eight sessions, March
        2024 : SEC adopts half-penny ticks, 18 September : immediately challenged in court
        2025 : Round lot tiers go live in November : D.C. Circuit denies the petition, 14 October
        2026 : Tick and access-fee compliance pushed to November 2027
